 "verdict": "THE LAYER CARRIES INFORMATION, WITHIN A LIMIT. Genes that are the sole or required catalyst of at least one reaction have dep_frac +0.0810 higher than genes whose reactions all have isozymes, after matching on PPI degree, reaction count and abundance (100% of 20 matched draws at p<0.05; 2.13x on the binarised label). Essentiality is not part of Human-GEM, so the catalytic-redundancy structure predicts something it was not built from; 81% of the raw +0.0997 survives matching. Adding publications to the matching moves it to +0.0845 (100% of draws p<0.05), so study effort is not what is producing it. BUT IT IS CONFINED: the effect lives in genes required for 1-2 reactions (+0.1090, 100% of draws p<0.05) and is not detectable for genes required for 3+ (+0.0207, only 35% of draws p<0.05), both matched against the same required-for-nothing group. The dose curve rises then falls, so the overall Spearman +0.1333 is positive only because the 0->1 step dominates the counts. Being required for MORE reactions does not make a gene more necessary. Those high-count genes sit disproportionately in replicated reaction families -- Transport reactions 31%, Fatty acid oxidation 11% -- where the count reflects how the model enumerates transport and acyl-chain chemistry rather than how many distinct jobs the gene holds."
}